  var ENO = {"homizy": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "Homizy was born from a radical choice: believing in compatibility, not in square meters. People still search for homes by size, neighborhood and price, but co-living doesn't fail for lack of space. It fails for lack of alignment.", "sc": 1, "st": [["65%", "of people name usual schedules"], ["47%", "name habits"], ["43%", "name how needs are communicated"]], "pts": [["Where it really breaks", "The gap is never the square meters. It is the alignment between people.", ["Communication", "Rhythms", "Schedules", "Habits"]]], "q": "“Co-living doesn't fail because of the spaces.”"}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "User profiling and room matching were built on two kinds of evidence: qualitative tools (benchmarks and interviews) and quantitative ones (surveys). One need came out clearly: a system capable of turning the roommate from a risk into a tangible enrichment.", "sc": 1, "st": [["67%", "practical compatibility"], ["33%", "social compatibility"]]}, {"h": "The Solution: ZyMatch & ZyMate", "t": "Homizy puts people at the center and turns housing into a shared experience built on affinity. The solution has a dual nature, enhancing compatibility through two distinct flows: ZyMatch & ZyMate.", "pts": [["1 · Practical flow", "During onboarding, users complete a questionnaire that profiles their habits and lets them define the type of roommate they are looking for. Matching criteria are weighted by importance, and the system generates three personalized housing proposals showing overall and detailed compatibility percentages.", ["Schedules &amp; cleanliness 25%", "Language 20%", "Sociality &amp; age 10%", "Culture, hobbies &amp; interests 5%"]], ["2 · Social flow", "Four archetypes define identity and belonging, turning a shared flat into a social ecosystem. After the assignment, users can explore their house right away or come back to it later from their profile. On its dedicated page they discover the house's characteristics and its personalized events. For people who move for work: a ready-made community, less isolation, easier networking and a home that helps them switch off.", ["Methodical", "Dreamers", "Trustworthy", "Passionate"], "inl"]], "q": "“Putting compatibility at the center of the experience.”"}, {"h": "The project in six questions", "t": "Six questions that frame the project, answered in short.", "pts": [["01 · The problem", "People still search for a shared home by size, neighborhood and price, but co-living breaks on communication, rhythms, schedules and habits. It affects people looking for a room and the roommates who will live with them."], ["02 · My contribution", "As UX/UI Designer intern (2026), I designed the end-to-end user flows for booking and shared-space interactions, built high-fidelity prototypes in Figma and iterated the interface for clarity and consistency across touchpoints."], ["03 · The insight", "Research showed that schedules (65%), habits (47%) and how needs are communicated (43%) weigh more than the spaces, and that compatibility is 67% practical and 33% social. The direction shifted from matching homes to matching people."], ["04 · The decision", "Instead of filters by size, neighborhood and price, I put compatibility at the center: a weighted questionnaire that returns three personalized proposals, plus four archetypes that give the social side an identity."], ["05 · The outcome", "A high-fidelity prototype of the full journey, from the onboarding questionnaire to ranked proposals with overall and detailed compatibility, and a system of four archetypes, ready to be validated with real users."], ["06 · What I learned", "With more time I would test the criteria weights with real roommates and check whether the archetypes help or limit matching. In social products, the logic behind a match has to be as clear as the interface."]]}]}, "antianti": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "In a landscape dominated by fast fashion and impulse buying, Generation Z doesn't like throwing away clothes they feel attached to, yet is used to replacing them the moment they get damaged.", "pts": [["The critical moment", "The instant a piece stops fitting the user's story."]], "q": "“In the 21st century, nothing is indispensable.”"}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "The issue is not lack of awareness. It's the value drop that happens when a garment gets damaged: in that moment the piece no longer supports the user's identity, and the easiest solution becomes buying something new.", "pts": [["The value drop", "A damaged garment stops expressing who the user is.", null, "tb"], ["The paradox", "Gen Z is very sensitive to sustainability, yet only sees the opportunity tied to consumption.", null, "tb"]], "q": "“Not a lack of awareness: a value drop.”"}, {"h": "The Solution: AntiAnti", "t": "A subscription-based digital service that intercepts this critical moment and turns it into an opportunity. Not a sewing tutorial, but a guided experience that helps users reinterpret and repair damaged clothes to make them desirable, and resellable, again.", "pts": [["Unlocking potential", "The value is not in perfection.<br>Inspired by the Japanese philosophy of Kintsugi, flaws become distinctive elements rather than defects to hide."], ["Gradual challenges", "Ideas, Personal and Sales sections let users take on gradual challenges, earn points and unlock more complex ones with high resale potential. It starts with accessible paths for heavily worn items like T-shirts."]], "q": "“It doesn't just teach how to fix clothes. It changes how value is perceived.”"}, {"h": "The project in six questions", "t": "Six questions that frame the project, answered in short.", "pts": [["01 · The problem", "Gen Z doesn't like throwing away clothes they feel attached to, yet replaces them the moment they get damaged. When a garment is stained, torn or loses its “postability”, it stops supporting its owner's identity."], ["02 · My contribution", "I designed the app concept and its main flows, from the defect scan to the gradual challenges and the hand-off to resale, up to an interactive Figma prototype."], ["03 · The insight", "The issue isn't lack of awareness. For Gen Z an object's value lies in its place within a personal narrative, and between breakage and consumption there is a moment nobody designs."], ["04 · The decision", "Instead of a sewing tutorial, a subscription service with guided paths that reinterpret the damage, inspired by Kintsugi, to make the piece desirable and resellable again. Selling is deliberately moved to Vinted."], ["05 · The outcome", "A prototype with three areas (Ideas, Personal, Sales), a defect-scan flow, challenges that unlock points and more complex paths, and a direct route to Vinted, starting with heavily worn T-shirts."], ["06 · What I learned", "With more time I would test the paths with real users to see whether they finish them and list the result, and extend beyond T-shirts. The value is not in perfection, but in unlocking potential."]]}]}, "perii": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "In Italy there are currently no dedicated digital solutions designed specifically for Alzheimer's patients, and even fewer addressing one of the most critical and dangerous symptoms: wandering.", "st": [["3M", "Alzheimer's caregivers in Italy, most of them related to the patient"]], "pts": [["A risk that can't be predicted", "Wandering is not just disorientation. It is an unpredictable behavior that exposes people with Alzheimer's to serious risks, and it leaves caregivers in a constant state of anxiety and hypervigilance: always alert, never able to relax, even when nothing is happening.", null, "tb"], ["A fear that stays", "It is a fear that stays: the thought of “what if they walk away?” often prevents families from leaving their loved ones alone, even for short periods of time. Even a quick errand becomes a source of worry.", null, "tb"]]}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "To understand the people behind the gap, the research combined interviews, desk research, field work and a benchmark. Caregivers are 62 years old on average and live wandering as a frequent thought, so the answer had to be simple and accessible.", "sc": 1, "st": [["8", "caregivers interviewed"], ["2", "neuropsychologists"], ["1", "care worker (OSS) in an Alzheimer's facility"], ["62", "average age of caregivers"]], "pts": [["How we listened", "Two interview scripts, one for caregivers and one for professionals, plus a full day in the field with a caregiver. With no Italian digital project to learn from, the benchmark looked abroad and at pet-tracking apps like Tractive.", ["Interviews", "Desk research", "Field research", "Benchmark"]]]}, {"h": "The Solution: PERII", "t": "PERII was designed to address this gap directly: a digital safety ecosystem that reduces the risks of wandering while supporting both patients and caregivers. At its core is a GPS-enabled wearable bracelet connected to a simple, accessible mobile app. The app is built around the caregiver: larger text and line spacing, simple concepts, attention to the thumb zone, a WhatsApp-like palette and a voice reader for those with vision problems.", "pts": [["Real-time location", "Caregivers can monitor the patient's location at any moment."], ["Safe zones", "Caregivers set predefined boundaries around places where the patient can move freely."], ["Instant alerts", "An alert arrives as soon as a boundary is crossed, with three choices: reach the person, call, or share their position with a relative."], ["A bracelet built around the patient", "An easy-to-change battery, in an AirTag-like system, avoids the stress of daily recharging. An elastic yet resistant material prevents easy removal."]], "q": "“PERII, where to find you again.”"}, {"h": "The project in six questions", "t": "Six questions that frame the project, answered in short.", "pts": [["01 · The problem", "In Italy there are no dedicated digital solutions for Alzheimer's, and even fewer for wandering, which exposes patients to serious risks and keeps caregivers in constant anxiety. About 3M caregivers are affected."], ["02 · My contribution", "I ran the research with caregivers and professionals, and designed the safety concept, the bracelet study and the app flows through to a prototype."], ["03 · The insight", "Caregivers are 62 on average and wandering is a frequent thought. No Italian digital project had taken off, so the benchmark came from abroad and from pet-tracking apps: the answer had to be simple and accessible."], ["04 · The decision", "A bracelet with an easy-to-change battery, to avoid the stress of daily recharging, and an elastic yet resistant material that prevents easy removal; an app with larger text, thumb-zone attention, a WhatsApp-like palette and a voice reader."], ["05 · The outcome", "A concept of the full ecosystem: onboarding that helps families make the bracelet acceptable, safe zones drawn on a map, instant alerts and an emergency phase with three choices: reach, call or share the position with a relative."], ["06 · What I learned", "With more time I would test the app with caregivers over 60 in real routines and verify GPS and battery in the field. In care, every extra step has an emotional cost."]]}]}, "lumen": {"d": "An AI agent, built with Tonidigrigio, that turns scattered team information into clarity.", "sec": [{"h": "The problem", "po": 1, "t": "It's Monday morning. A project is already moving fast, but no one has a clear picture: ask two people and you get two different answers.", "pts": [["Which task should be prioritized?", "It depends on who you ask.", null, "flat"], ["Where's the latest client feedback?", "Somewhere between Slack, Drive, emails and meeting notes.", null, "flat"]], "st": [["67%", "of the team doesn't know how much budget remains on a project"], ["73%", "say initial estimates are exceeded by 30–50%"], ["56%", "experience shifting priorities at least three times a week"], ["40%", "of working documents never reach the entire team"]]}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "This isn't a people problem. It's an information problem that needs to be fixed. Where does the time go? Time is spent searching, aligning and catching up instead of creating value. That is why we need to requalify how we work: in 2026, work habits and environments need to be requalified to improve the quality of tasks, and the quality of the team's actions.", "q": "“It isn't a people problem. It's an information problem.”"}, {"h": "The Solution: Lumen", "t": "Now imagine an AI agent embedded directly into the team's workflow, built in collaboration with Tonidigrigio. Not another tool to learn, but a teammate, like a kind senior colleague: it connects information, tracks project health, surfaces risks and delivers answers before people have to ask. <b>The agent speaks up</b>: less searching, less uncertainty, better decisions:", "ch": ["“18 hours remaining.”", "“Priority changed this morning.”", "“Budget overrun risk detected.”"], "pts": [["Ask Lumen", "One question, many sources. Lumen searches chats, documents, emails and files, links the information and answers with the sources to check, like “What do I need to know before today's call?”"], ["Projects at a glance", "A dashboard with the day's priorities, project status, your tasks and risk signals, such as a budget at 92% with three weeks left."], ["Workflow and decisions", "Workflow tracking shows what is in progress, waiting or blocked, while a decision log records priority changes and a timeline estimates iterations."], ["Workload", "A workload view warns of overload months and lets Lumen help resolve it."]], "q": "“Not to work harder, but to work with clarity, in a smoother way.”"}, {"h": "The project in six questions", "t": "Six questions that frame the project, answered in short.", "pts": [["01 · The problem", "Teams juggling several projects lose time because budget, priorities and client feedback are scattered across Slack, Drive, emails and meeting notes. It affects project teams and whoever coordinates them."], ["02 · My contribution", "Together with Tonidigrigio, I designed the experience of the AI agent: the concept, the main screens (dashboard, search, project workflow, workload) and the interactive Figma prototype."], ["03 · The insight", "67% of the team doesn't know how much budget remains, 73% see estimates exceeded by 30–50%, 56% face shifting priorities every week and 40% of documents never reach everyone. It isn't a people problem, it's an information problem."], ["04 · The decision", "Rather than adding another tool to learn, the agent lives inside the workflow and speaks first. It connects sources, tracks project health and surfaces risks, and every answer shows the sources it comes from."], ["05 · The outcome", "A prototype with a daily dashboard of priorities and risk signals, a search that answers with cited sources, workflow and decision tracking, and a workload view that flags overload."], ["06 · What I learned", "With more time I would test the alerts with real teams to find the line between useful and noisy, and measure the time saved on searching. Designing for AI means designing trust: sources, tone and timing matter as much as the answer."]]}]}};
